===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = Creneau.XVe.siecle.png
|caption1 = An illustration of a [[battlement]] or [[crenellation]].{{refn|group=n|name=n1|From {{cite-book|author=Eugène Viollet-le-Duc|authorlink=Eugène Viollet-le-Duc|entry=CRÉNEAU|title=Dictionnaire raisonné de l’architecture française du XI{{sup|e}} au XVI{{sup|e}} siècle|trans-title=Systematic Dictionary of French Architecture from the 9th to 16th Centuries|location=Paris|publisher=B. Bance,{{nb...|éditeur, rue Bonaparte, 13.}}|year=1858|volume=IV (Construction–Cyborium)|section=figure 15|page=387|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dictionnaireraisbyu04viol/page/387/mode/1up|oclc=458608792}}.}} The merlons are the [[upright#Adjective|upright]] [[projecting#Adjective|projecting]] [[part#Noun|parts]], and the portions in between them are the [[crenel]]s or [[embrasure]]s. [[loophole|Loopholes]] are present in the merlons depicted.
|image2 = Chess rlt26.svg
|caption2 = This [[symbol]] of a [[castle#Noun|castle]] or [[rook]] in [[chess]] has three merlons at its top.
|image3 = Köln römische Stadtmauer - Turm 13 (Römerturm).jpg
|caption3 = Merlons [[encircle|encircling]] the top of one of the [[tower#Noun|towers]] of the old [[Roman#Adjective|Roman]] [[city]] [[wall#Noun|wall]] of [[Cologne]], [[North Rhine-Westphalia]], [[Germany]].
}}

{{PIE root|en|h₂ems}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|merlon}}, from {{der|en|it|merlone|t=merlon}}, from {{m|it|merlo|t=merlon}} + {{m|it|-one|pos=suffix forming {{glossary|augmentative}}s}}. ''Merlo'' is derived from {{der|en|LL.|merulus}}, {{m|la|merlus}}, possibly from {{der|en|la|merula|t=blackbird}} (as merlons resemble a row of birds perched on a wall),<ref name="OED">Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=116825|date=September 2001|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂ems-|t=black; blackbird}}. The English word is cognate with {{cog|it|mergolo|t=battlement; pinnacle}}, {{cog|pt|merlão}}, {{cog|es|merlón|t=merlon}}.<ref name="OED"/>

